        faq1Question: "What course formats are offered?",
        faq1Answer1: "Our courses offer you a flexible learning experience:",
        faq1Answer2: "<strong>Hybrid Education:</strong>",
        faq1Answer3: "Your course consists of a combination of face-to-face classes and live online classes.",
        faq1Answer4: "Thanks to the hybrid system, you can also attend classes online whenever you want on face-to-face class days. Our classrooms have technical equipment that allows instructors, in-class students, and online participants to interact simultaneously.",
        faq1Answer5: "<strong>Live Online Education:</strong>",
        faq1Answer6: "In this format, you attend classes completely live and online.",
        faq1Answer7: "You can attend live online classes from your home, while out, or from our centers. You can benefit from the learning areas and technical facilities (tablet and iPad rental, printer, copier and scanner use) at our education centers in Düsseldorf and Berlin. You also have the opportunity to meet other participants from different countries around the world in our cafe or during social activities.",
        faq2Question: "What content is covered in the courses?",
        faq2Answer: "Our Intensive German programs include listening and reading comprehension with current texts, comprehensive grammar studies, and various communication-focused activities. This aims to develop your speaking and writing skills in a goal-oriented manner.",
        faq3Question: "How can I get university education in Germany?",
        faq3Answer1: "To receive university education in Germany, you must first have recognized university entrance qualification (HZB). It is recommended to check whether your diploma is recognized in Germany through the anabin system.",
        faq3Answer2: "You also need to document your German language proficiency with official certificates such as telc, DSH. The Campus German University Preparation Program is designed for you to prepare for this process completely.",
        faq3Answer3: "Candidates who do not have direct university admission rights must first complete preparatory education called Studienkolleg and successfully pass the Feststellungsprüfung exam. After this exam, you can register for a suitable undergraduate program. We support you step by step in this process with our academic counseling service.",
        faq3Answer4: "University applications are generally made through universities' own systems or through the central application platform Uni-Assist. Students from non-EU countries are required to obtain a student visa. Valid health insurance is also required.",
        faq3Answer5: "In addition, you need to prove that you can cover your education and living expenses. After receiving acceptance, it is sufficient to complete your university registration and submit the necessary documents.",
        faq4Question: "What requirements are necessary?",
        faq4Answer1: "You must be at least 18 years old",
        faq4Answer2: "You must submit a blocked account opened in Germany or an official commitment letter",
        faq5Question: "What types of visas are recommended?",
        faq5Answer1: "<strong>Student Visa:</strong>",
        faq5Answer2: "Also covers the right to participate in preparatory German courses.",
        faq5Answer3: "There is no visa requirement for EU citizens.",
        faq6Question: "What services are included in the courses?",
        faq6Answer1: "Placement test",
        faq6Answer2: "No registration fee",
        faq6Answer3: "Course certificate for visa application",
        faq6Answer4: "Digital textbook",
        faq6Answer5: "Individual student counseling (1 time, approximately 45 minutes)",
        faq6Answer6: "Webinars on 'University Education in Germany'",
        faq6Answer7: "Campus German certificate",
        faq6Answer8: "E-learning platform",
        faq6Answer9: "Social and cultural activities organized throughout the year",
        faq6Answer10: "Free Wi-Fi access at all centers",
        faq7Question: "What are the qualifications of our instructors?",
        faq7Answer1: "Our teaching staff consists of university graduates with many years of teaching experience in German as a Foreign Language (DaF) and German as a Second Language (DaZ), experts in their field, and highly motivated people.",
        faq7Answer2: "We also have Federal Office for Migration and Refugees (BAMF) approved instructors. Our lessons are regularly evaluated by students.",
        faq8Question: "How long does it take to learn German?",
        faq8Answer: "German learning time depends on your goal and current level. You can complete the basic levels from A1 to B2 in 6-8 months. Advanced levels such as C1 and C2 may require an additional 4-6 months. You can shorten this time with our intensive courses.",
        faq9Question: "Can I get a Goethe certificate?",
        faq9Answer: "Yes, we offer Goethe certificate preparation courses at Campus German. We provide exam preparation at all levels from A1 to C2. You can take certificate exams at our campus or be directed to nearby test centers.",
        faq10Question: "Are accommodation options available?",
        faq10Answer: "Yes, we offer accommodation options in Germany. Student dormitories, homestay accommodation, and shared apartments are available. Our accommodation service is not included in the course fee, but we help you find the most suitable options.",
        faq11Question: "Are payment plans and discounts available?",
        faq11Answer: "Yes, we offer flexible payment plans. Monthly, 3-month, and 6-month payment options are available. There are discounts for early registration, group registrations, and long-term programs. Contact us for detailed information.",
